{#- The six questions from their FAQ (infloww.com, captured site.captureDate), answered for this repo.
    Native <details>, no JS. Shared by the landing page and, later, /pricing/. -#}
{%- macro faqItem(question) -%}
<details class="group py-2">
  <summary class="flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-4 py-3 text-lg font-medium text-ink">
    {{ question }}
    <svg class="size-5 shrink-0 text-muted group-open:rotate-45 motion-safe:transition-transform" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="#i-plus" /></svg>
  </summary>
  <div class="space-y-3 pb-4 text-muted">{{ caller() }}</div>
</details>
{%- endmacro %}
<section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title" class="wrap py-20 sm:py-28">
  <p class="eyebrow">Help</p>
  <h2 id="faq-title" class="section-title mt-3">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="mt-10 divide-y divide-line border-y border-line">
    {% call faqItem("Why do I need Inflowwww?") %}
      <p>
        You might not. It's built for teams with someone who can rent a server and paste commands into a
        terminal. If that's you and you'd rather not pay per creator, it gives you Messages Pro, Smart Messages,
        Vault Pro, Scripts, creator, employee and fan reports, and Auto Post and Auto Story, on your own server.
        Some screens, like OF Manager and Share for Share, are still NotBuiltYet; the
        <a class="text-link" href="{{ '/roadmap/' | url }}">roadmap</a> lists them.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you'd rather not run a server,
        <a class="text-link" href="{{ '/openinflowwww-vs-infloww/' | url }}#stay">here is who should stay on Infloww</a>.
      </p>
    {% endcall %}
    {% call faqItem("What makes Inflowwww different from other platforms?") %}
      <p>
        It isn't a platform. It is MIT-licensed code you run yourself, so no vendor sits between you and your
        creators' accounts, and nobody else holds your data.
      </p>
    {% endcall %}
    {% call faqItem("Is Inflowwww safe?") %}
      <p>
        We make no safety promises. Inflowwww is an independent reverse-engineering of OnlyFans' public web
        API, and running it against a live account may violate OnlyFans' Terms of Service. Use it at your own risk.
      </p>
      <p>
        What we can say: sessions and the database stay on your server, and you can read every line of code that
        touches them. Access is a share token for the dashboard plus per-user logins for the owner (at
        <code>/login</code>) and each chatter (at <code>/chatter-login</code>); chatters don't need the share token.
      </p>
      <p>
        Today the relay (the background service that talks to OnlyFans) lets anyone who reaches it register, with no
        setting and no share token, and any registered account can list every user and creator account. It stores
        the owner's and chatters' dashboard passwords in plain text (creators' OnlyFans passwords never reach it),
        the lanes panel (the relay's rate-limit page at <code>/lanes-panel</code>) can change its limits and restart it
        with no login, <code>ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> alone unlocks
        "view as", transfer and delete, and logins have no rate limit. Run it only on a private network or over a VPN
        (WireGuard or Tailscale) until these are fixed; they are listed under
        <a class="text-link" href="{{ '/roadmap/' | url }}#known-gaps">Known gaps</a> on the roadmap, and the
        <a class="text-link" href="{{ '/help/#security' | url }}">security notes</a> say what to do if you must expose it.
      </p>
    {% endcall %}
    {% call faqItem("How much does Inflowwww cost?") %}
      <p>
        $0, for any number of creators at any earnings. You pay for your server, your proxies (you supply them;
        none are built in) and, if you add LLM keys, the provider's usage.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm">
        Theirs: <q>Starting at as little as $40 a month…</q> (infloww.com FAQ, captured <span class="whitespace-nowrap">{{ site.captureDate }}</span>).
      </p>
    {% endcall %}
    {% call faqItem("What metrics can I track?") %}
      <p>
        {#- First-use glosses: the landing spells out PPV in its feature list and session capture here;
            /pricing/ spells out session capture on the Fansly card and PPV here. -#}
        {%- set ppv = " (PPVs, tips and messages)." if page.url == "/" else ": pay-per-view (PPV) messages, tips and other messages." %}
        Whatever the Creator, Employee and Fan reports and the Message dashboard show{{ ppv }}
        It all sits in one SQLite file on your disk, so you can query anything else yourself.
      </p>
    {% endcall %}
    {% call faqItem("How do I get started?") %}
      <p>
        Follow the <a class="text-link" href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/README.md#quick-start">README Quick start</a>:
        create the Docker network (<code>docker network create n8n_default</code>), the state files and an
        <code>.env</code> with a <code>SHARE_TOKEN</code>, then run <code>docker compose up -d --build</code>. The
        <a class="text-link" href="{{ '/#deploy' | url }}">deploy section on the home page</a> has all eight commands. (The network name comes from the
        maintainer's own Traefik setup; you don't need n8n.) Register the owner at <code>/login</code>, then
        capture an OnlyFans session{{ " (signing in to OnlyFans once in Chrome so the relay can act on the account)" if page.url == "/" }}
        with the Chrome extension in <code>loginExtensionMulti/</code> (load it in
        <code>chrome://extensions</code> with Developer mode &gt; Load unpacked). The desktop app's own capture
        button does not work against this relay today; use the Chrome extension.
      </p>
    {% endcall %}
  </div>
</section>
